module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.wet_witness_premise_read_from_the_runner_it_happens_to_land_on

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, decl_ref }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data wet_witness_premise_read_from_the_runner_it_happens_to_land_on: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "wet_witness_premise_read_from_the_runner_it_happens_to_land_on" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a wet witness's premise read from the runner it happens to land on** (a required-floor claim is ABOUT a host of one kind -- here, an executor with no event log placement -- but observes the executing runner's identity instead of supplying the subject, so its expected verdict is true only on the runners that happen to be of that kind; on any other runner it is red with no change to the code).",

    "SPECIMEN (2026-09-27, #12425 and other unrelated PRs). test.claim.spark.fabric_capacity_standing_wet_witness and test.claim.spark.spark_pair_serving_apply_wet_witness observed the runner's hostname and asserted every group's authority read refused 'off fleet'. The floor job passed on srv3/srv4 runners and failed (expected passed, observed failed) on srv1 runners: gunbc.roadmap_dashboard_instance declares a dashboard instance for srv1, so gunbc.fabric_event_log_host event_log_store_for_host RESOLVES there and the premise is false. Not a regression in the read and not flake: the verdict was a function of runner assignment.",

    "WHY IT IS A CLASS: a CI runner pool is not a neutral environment -- runners are fleet hosts, and which one a job lands on is scheduling, not an input the claim declares. The tell is a wet claim whose expectation names a property of the host ('off fleet', 'no dashboard', 'no GPU') while its subject is taken from observe_executor_reach or another observation of the executing machine. DESIGN §3: the subject belongs at the interface as a supplied value.",

    "REPAIR (gunbc#12478, landed; this row was filed by gunbc#12461, whose parallel repair was superseded by it): the off-fleet premise is a SUPPLIED value at the entry's seam, gunbc.instruments.fabric_capacity_standing fabric_capacity_standing_for, and at spark_pair_apply_plans, with a host that declares no dashboard instance and a fixed instant. The store resolution and the per-group refusal naming the read still run for real on every runner. #12478 also kept two claims on the RUNNER'S OWN host that joined the verdict against whatever the runner's log read returned; the next receipt removes them. RUNG found at 1 (the harm occurred: unrelated PRs and the merge queue blocked). NOW 2 for these claims. CEILING 2: whether a wet claim's expectation depends on a host fact is not yet a modeled fact of the wet roster. NEXT TRIGGER, capability not artifact: the wet lane schedule records, per scheduled claim, which host facts its verdict depends on, and refuses a claim whose expectation depends on a host fact the runner pool does not hold uniformly.",

    "TRIM (2026-09-28, operator direction: wet witnesses may execute for real but must not depend on active host or fleet state; the CI runners ARE fleet hosts). The two runner's-own-host claims read the live event log twice and compared the answers, so they still depended on active state: a transition landing between the reads reddened them, and by their own stated ceiling they could not tell a real read from a resting-row fallback while a group's log still equals its row. They are deleted with their wet-schedule rows and route-gap expectations. The two supplied-host claims reach no host effect, so they move into their hermetic siblings, test.claim.spark.fabric_capacity_standing_witness and test.claim.spark.spark_pair_serving_apply_witness; the wet files, their four wet-schedule rows and their two LocalRepoWetLane exclusion rows are deleted. No witness executes observe_executor_reach for these entries any more: the executor's name is host state by nature, and it is the part that made the verdict a function of runner placement.",

    "SECOND SPECIMEN (2026-09-29, #12434 floor run 36597893469 attempt 2, runner srv1-09). test.claim.approval_ntfy_access_readback_wet_witness_test the_root_refuses_before_minting_on_this_host_by_real_execution required that gunbc.auth.approval_device_redemption approval_device_store_root NOT exist on the runner -- a host premise, and srv1 falsified it once the root was created there (by hand, then by approval_device_store_directory). The run's log leads with `sudo: a password is required` from every as_ntfy_server read and the per-release helper, and a sibling's `cat /proc/<pid>/environ: Permission denied`; those are NOT the cause. The verb reads them as failed reads and refuses before minting, which is exactly the verdict the claim expects on any host (gunbc.auth.approval_ntfy_access_readback approval_ntfy_channel_verdict, first_failed_read), and the sibling that met the environ denial passed. What reddened the claim was the store-absence conjunct. The repair had already landed as gunbc#12614 (the claim compares the store's existence before and after the call instead of assuming it absent); #12434's pull_request floor judged its own head, which predates #12614, so it still carried the host premise. Integrating main into the PR is the whole remedy there. THE TELL, kept: a wet claim's red log lists host-dependent read failures first; read the claim's conjunctions against the verdict before attributing the red to the loudest line, because a refusal the verb takes on a failed read is the expected verdict, not the defect.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.instruments.fabric_capacity_standing", decl_name: "fabric_capacity_standing_for"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric_event_log_host", decl_name: "event_log_store_for_host"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.approval_ntfy_access_readback_wet_witness_test", decl_name: "the_root_refuses_before_minting_on_this_host_by_real_execution"),
  ],
}
